      "message": "yes, but i\u0027ll share in a PM.  the goal isnt directly performance in this CL;  \n\nToday\u0027s arm hardware implements sve and neon with the same vector units, on the arm cores.  There are only minor instruction differences, where sve has a few instructions that are not exposed in neon, but most code would be 100% 1:1 the same.\n\nThe predicates and threat of VL changing means the sve row functions handle the remainder themselves, which is easy most cases - convert the \u0027width\u0027 to a predicate and use that with a duplicate copy of the main loop.\nThe function to create the predicate is slow on Cortex A510, so we only do it if there is a remainder.\n\nThe purpose of this CL is ground work for SSVE.  The exact same SVE code can be called in \u0027streaming mode\u0027, but the smstart/smstop are slow (50 cycles), so that overhead kills performance if done on every row.  Especially on multi-step conversions.  So hoisting the smstart/stop to once per image found eliminates that drawback:\n\nARGBToNV12\nImplementation │ Benchmark Time (ms) │ smstart/smstop Switches\n───────────────┼─────────────────────┼─────────────────────────\nSME Per-Frame  │      1,376 ms       │   1/image (1,000 total)\nSME Per-Row    │      1,689 ms       │ 360/image (360,000 total)",
